WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.434
How a Mechanical

00:00:01.246 --> 00:00:02.378
Watch Keeps Time

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:04.384
An interactive 3D

00:00:04.195 --> 00:00:05.292
explainer of how

00:00:05.103 --> 00:00:06.235
a mechanical watch

00:00:06.047 --> 00:00:06.919
keeps time

00:00:07.058 --> 00:00:09.345
7½ times 8 is 60

00:00:09.452 --> 00:00:10.230
so the center

00:00:10.042 --> 00:00:10.996
wheel turns once

00:00:10.808 --> 00:00:11.515
an hour

00:00:12.046 --> 00:00:13.225
Mechanical watches

00:00:13.037 --> 00:00:13.991
have kept time

00:00:13.803 --> 00:00:15.454
for 500 years

00:00:15.593 --> 00:00:17.821
In 1675 Christiaan

00:00:17.633 --> 00:00:18.929
Huygens a Dutch

00:00:18.741 --> 00:00:19.637
scientist

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:21.135
invented the hairspring

00:00:21.277 --> 00:00:22.231
In this drawing

00:00:22.043 --> 00:00:22.986
it swings once

00:00:22.869 --> 00:00:24.083
every two seconds

00:00:24.142 --> 00:00:24.955
and the rest

00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:25.439
of the watch

00:00:25.251 --> 00:00:26.429
follows that beat

00:00:26.966 --> 00:00:28.157
I personally think

00:00:27.968 --> 00:00:29.053
it’s fascinating that

00:00:28.865 --> 00:00:29.996
a well-calibrated

00:00:30.174 --> 00:00:31.694
purely mechanical device

00:00:31.506 --> 00:00:32.461
can keep time

00:00:32.273 --> 00:00:35.232
with 99.99% accuracy

00:00:35.044 --> 00:00:36.553
for $100

00:00:37.086 --> 00:00:38.100
Full story on

00:00:37.911 --> 00:00:38.807
the dashboard
